{"id": "sevt_01VRXHPdEDp7gVALruX1dWE6", "content": [{"text": "Confirmed. The per-row query first appears at 14:22:31 \u2014 right after the 14:22:30 deploy \u2014 and there are zero before it. Multiple `order_items` queries share one `request_id` (`req_5b5ffdb97cc9`), which is the N+1 fan-out.\n\n**Evidence**\n- checkout p99: flat 66\u201380ms through 14:21, jumps to 3404ms in the 14:22 bucket, climbs to ~5800ms by 14:59.\n- Deploy `6db1b9b` (checkout, alice) landed 14:22:30 \u2014 exact alignment with the jump. Error rate stays low (~0\u20130.7), so this is latency, not failures \u2192 slow query, not an outage.\n- Diff replaces a single batched `OrderItem` query (`order_id__in=ids`) with a per-order query inside a `for` loop \u2192 N+1.\n- Log confirms: per-row `SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ?` queries start at 14:22:31 (none before), many per single `request_id`.\n- Red herrings ruled out: cart `50293ce` at 14:21:05 *adds* backoff (improvement, not a cause) and cart isn't the paged service; auth deploy is 2.5h earlier.\n\n> **Root cause:** `6db1b9b` \u2014 the order-summary refactor turned one batched `OrderItem` lookup into a per-row query inside the loop (N+1), so checkout p99 blew up right after the 14:22:30 deploy.\n\nFix: revert `6db1b9b` or restore the batched `order_id__in` query + in-memory grouping.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:30:00.471046Z", "type": "agent.message"}
